<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolt</p><h1 class="enc-title">Constitutionalist Revolution</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1932">1932</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Paulista War</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Constitutionalist Revolution was an armed uprising of the state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sao-paulo">São Paulo</a> against the provisional government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/getulio-vargas">Getúlio Vargas</a>, fought from July to October 1932. The rebels demanded elections and a new constitution after Vargas had seized power in 1930 and appointed federal interventors to run the states. They were defeated militarily, but Vargas called a constituent assembly, and a new constitution was adopted in 1934.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/constitutionalist-revolution.jpg" alt="Constitutionalist Revolution" width="320" height="226" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Schneider-Canet_150mm_na_Revolu%C3%A7%C3%A3o_de_1932.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The Revolution of 1930 brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/getulio-vargas">Getúlio Vargas</a> to the presidency with the backing of the military and the political elites of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minas-gerais">Minas Gerais</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-grande-do-sul">Rio Grande do Sul</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paraiba">Paraíba</a>. It overthrew President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/washington-luis">Washington Luís</a>, kept the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sao-paulo">São Paulo</a> governor Júlio Prestes, elected president in 1930, from taking office, and ended the First Brazilian Republic. Paulistas resented the loss of the autonomy the states had held under the 1891 constitution. The federal government accused the movement of seeking secession, but there is no evidence that its commanders were separatists.</p><p>On 23 May 1932 government troops killed four protesting students, Martins, Miragaia, Dráusio and Camargo, whose initials gave the movement its name, MMDC; a fifth, Alvarenga, died months later. The uprising began on 9 July 1932.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sao-paulo">São Paulo</a>'s politicians expected a quick war with the support of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minas-gerais">Minas Gerais</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-grande-do-sul">Rio Grande do Sul</a>, which never came. According to Javier García de Gabiola, the rebels won over only one of the army's eight divisions, the 2nd Division in São Paulo, reinforced by the Força Pública Paulista and MMDC militias. With 15,000 to 29,000 rifles, depending on the source, they could never arm more than 35,000 men, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> equipped about 100,000.</p><p>The 2nd Division's advance on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-janeiro">Rio de Janeiro</a> was stopped in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paraiba-valley">Paraíba Valley</a> by the loyalist 1st Division under General Góis Monteiro, and three months of trench warfare followed. In the south federal forces broke the rebel line at Itararé on 17 July, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minas-gerais">Minas Gerais</a> front, active from 2 August, proved decisive: federal troops broke through at Eleutério on 26 August and came within 70 kilometres of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sao-paulo">São Paulo</a>. The Paulistas surrendered on 2 October to General Valdomiro Lima. There were 934 official deaths, though unofficial estimates reach 2,200.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Despite the defeat, Vargas granted several of the movement's main demands: a non-military state governor, the election of a Constituent Assembly and a new constitution in 1934. That constitution was short-lived. In 1937 Vargas dissolved the National Congress and, after a coup d'état, enacted another that established the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estado-novo-portugal">Estado Novo</a>. The uprising was the first major revolt against the Vargas government, and 9 July, the day it began, is a holiday and the most important civic date of the state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sao-paulo">São Paulo</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Constitutionalist Revolution” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>São Paulo rose in the <b>Constitutionalist Revolution</b> of 1932, which was defeated.</blockquote><a href="/state/second-brazilian-republic">Second Brazilian Republic</a></li></ul></section>
